#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083039 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/id #uuid "cdd74ee4-b82f-520f-8541-f328f9e63fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8279" :question/problem "The birth weights of newborns in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $3\\,543$ grams and a standard deviation of $609$ grams. What is the probability that a randomly selected newborn baby weighs more than $3\\,100$ grams but less than $3\\,600$? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| | $z$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2033 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2327 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2643 \\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4207 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5319 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5359 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5714 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5753 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2033 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2327 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2643 \\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4207 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5319 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5359 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5714 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5753 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the weight of a randomly selected baby. Then we have $X \\sim N(3\\,543, 609^2).$\n\nWe want to find $P(3\\,100 <   X <  3\\,600).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\,100<X<3\\,600) & =P(\\frac{3\\,100-3\\,543}{609}<Z<\\frac{3\\,600-3\\,543}{609}) \\\\  & =P(-0.73<Z<0.09)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.73<Z<0.09) & =P(Z<0.09)-P(Z<-0.73) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.09)-P(Z\\le -0.73) \\\\  & =Φ(0.09)-Φ(-0.73)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.73) & =0.2327 \\\\ Φ(0.09) & =0.5359\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.73<Z<0.09) & =Φ(0.09)-Φ(-0.73) \\\\  & =0.5359-0.2327 \\\\  & =0.3032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the probability that a randomly selected newborn baby weighs more than $3\\,100$ grams but less than $3\\,600$ is $0.30.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/id #uuid "5905ffa9-e7e2-5288-8714-91357fb17139" :question/math-academy-id "e-8280" :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the episodes of a popular podcast is $43\\,\\textrm{min}.$ Additionally, $20\\%$ of the episodes have a duration of less than $38$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of a randomly selected episode is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the episode durations, rounded to the nearest minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ | $\\, 0.15 \\,$ | $\\, 0.20 \\,$ | $\\, 0.25 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, -2.3263 \\,$ | $\\, -1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, -1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, -1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, -1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, -0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, -0.6745 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly selected episode. Then, we have $X \\sim N(43, \\sigma^2),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma.$ \n\nWe know that $P(X \\leq 38) = 0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 38) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{38-43}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{5}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.20) =-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1683 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.1683} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈6\\,\\text{minutes.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "4049eb36-a8b0-5ef7-bf82-6f38cbe5cb17" :question/math-academy-id "e-8281" :question/problem "The systolic blood pressure of a group of people is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $12\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ Additionally, $50\\%$ of the people have a systolic blood pressure between $0.93\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and $1.07\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$. Find, as a percentage, the proportion of people with a systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.75 \\,$ | $\\, 0.80 \\,$ | $\\, 0.85 \\,$ | $\\, 0.90 \\,$ | $\\, 0.95 \\,$ | $\\, 0.97 \\,$ | $\\, 0.99 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, 0.6745 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, 1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, 1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, 1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, 1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, 2.3263 \\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p = \\Phi(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6026 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6406 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the systolic blood pressure of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X \\sim N(\\mu, 12^2),$ and we want to find $P(X <  120).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu.$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.93\\mu  <   X <   1.07\\mu ) = 0.5.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.93\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.25 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5+0.25 \\\\ P(X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.75 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12}) & =0.75 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12}) & =0.75 \\\\ \\frac{0.07\\mu }{12} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.75).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.75) \\approx 0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12} & =0.6745 \\\\ 0.07\\mu  & =8.094 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.094}{0.07} \\\\  & ≈115.629.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $P(X <   120)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<120) & =P(Z<\\frac{120-115.629}{12}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.36) \\\\  & =Φ(0.36) \\\\  & =0.6406 \\\\  & ≈64.1%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $64.1\\%$ of the people have systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083058 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"}] :question/id #uuid "c55db5a3-2d8c-57d7-b860-8628cb8738f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9892" :question/problem "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070457/assets/d40721092d0e11fdc2ac582688491a922bd0c6240e5bfa44b6374a73f6577685.png)" :question/worked-solution "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is shown in diagram II:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070457/assets/382e3bc616bc15ac99fb5f18f1f9a75925eb2b5760152c7088fdbc7047fff904.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f169b3dc-07e1-5561-b44c-145ccbc91899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366840} {:answer/id #uuid "d422fde6-5a1d-5dfb-9963-cdac5919e108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366841} {:answer/id #uuid "26175838-efe2-56b5-aadc-6f37324df82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366842} {:answer/id #uuid "da8374ea-7146-5037-afb1-4fcbd9c1aec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366843} {:answer/id #uuid "59f17cf5-492c-5be7-91c9-2060fd7a87f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59f17cf5-492c-5be7-91c9-2060fd7a87f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366844} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb79ea7-0075-5837-927e-2be2a361f053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73d7efe3-40f0-50bf-94e8-8fb8e2a025f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132712" :question/problem "In a factory, the amount of sugar on a cookie is normally distributed with a mean of $15$ grams and a standard deviation of $0.8$ grams. What is the probability that the amount of sugar on a randomly selected cookie is less than $14$ grams? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ | $\\,0.1492\\,$ | $\\,0.1469\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the amount of sugar (in grams) on the cookies. Then we have $X∼N(15,{0.8}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<14).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<14)=P(X\\le 14)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 14) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{14-15}{0.8}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.25)=0.1056≈0.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the amount of sugar on a randomly selected cookie is less than $14$ grams is approximately $0.11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cdc2d3e-95b2-5055-8968-927437d8da0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366847} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ac9d11-eacf-56f7-93fd-ebde418cf51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.74$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366848} {:answer/id #uuid "60c04aee-7859-5b3a-b732-65288ee8345a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.65$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366849} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ab57a3-32ef-5d3a-ac3f-674533b88ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.57$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366850} {:answer/id #uuid "489cc168-b1ab-5540-b902-b1e06d3ab1e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.79$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cdc2d3e-95b2-5055-8968-927437d8da0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366847} :answer-field/id #uuid "b88c9f33-1bd4-5226-9952-5607a85200c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42e998b4-da64-533d-80e4-6303495d71cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132852" :question/problem "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years.\nAdditionally, $3%$ of batteries last less than $1.75$ years. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<1.75)=0.03.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.75) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)=-1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.8808 \\\\ 1.75-\\mu  & =-0.9404 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈2.69.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c848ccc-179b-57e9-82d4-ba54ed3a5d81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.27$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366854} {:answer/id #uuid "986983e8-eff1-586a-9b17-e1a646ae66c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.73$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366855} {:answer/id #uuid "169778b1-f30d-506c-9b79-388d478ba232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4.85$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366856} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ede810-33f1-565d-81ce-b556572146bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4.41$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366857} {:answer/id #uuid "81a10ef6-7e42-5825-9004-f402e04dd26f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.38$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c848ccc-179b-57e9-82d4-ba54ed3a5d81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.27$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366854} :answer-field/id #uuid "d28733a3-72ae-5e83-8386-01017c625f20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50c01113-7eba-59c9-b302-374966a56f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-132856" :question/problem "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.7.$ In addition, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.75\\mu $ and $$1.25\\mu .$ Find the average cinema admission fee, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent an admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & =1.8808 \\\\ 0.25\\mu  & =1.31656 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.31656}{0.25} \\\\  & ≈5.27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6b5ac2c-3640-55e5-9b37-6d859a73cb5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366861} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b0b6eb-b2a0-526b-9417-9c21d1b399ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "73.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366862} {:answer/id #uuid "78d457d5-ae68-59ca-8ab4-ee2f3ba32db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366863} {:answer/id #uuid "66a69e35-cd9b-5f62-b76a-e8f71b548f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366864} {:answer/id #uuid "f571f37a-f2a6-5948-a5af-f4a701a54f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78d457d5-ae68-59ca-8ab4-ee2f3ba32db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366863} :answer-field/id #uuid "44264643-2cfb-5e42-897c-377536cb8eab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23fbfc78-d3e6-58a5-85fb-02e846b95199" :question/math-academy-id "q-132860" :question/problem "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.75.$ Additionally, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.7\\mu $ and $$1.3\\mu .$ Find the percentage of cases when the admission fee is lower than $$5.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent a randomly selected admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.75}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<5).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97) \\\\ 0.4\\mu  & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.4\\mu  & =1.8808 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.8808}{0.4} \\\\  & ≈4.702.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(Z<\\frac{5-4.702.}{0.75}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.6554 \\\\  & ≈65.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately in $65.5%$ of cases, the admission fee is lower than $$5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c39c204c-5f5d-5cdf-9fc7-3f62344cd7ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366868} {:answer/id #uuid "2e42cddd-68a6-5a67-bf44-5d2b270707d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366869} {:answer/id #uuid "109d914c-8671-5252-98f3-d900c3d60264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366870} {:answer/id #uuid "83940ba6-93cc-541f-8381-de1561768377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366871} {:answer/id #uuid "5f060a3b-332c-5c3a-922d-4256f29a604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83940ba6-93cc-541f-8381-de1561768377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366871} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee088655-a3b2-59e4-a930-adc3c565f0b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45137841-ca0a-5be6-86ab-33c4b038822e" :question/math-academy-id "q-133053" :question/problem "The diameter of the kibble of a particular cat food brand is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{mm}.$ Additionally, the diameter of $20%$ of kibbles is less than $4.4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter. Find the percentage of kibbles whose diameter is less than $4\\,\\text{mm},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0808\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the diameter of a randomly selected kibble. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the $P(X<4).$\n\nWe know that $P(X<4.4)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4.4) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 4.4-\\mu  & =-0.58912 \\\\ \\mu  & =4.98912\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{4-4.98912}{0.7}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.41) \\\\  & =0.0793 \\\\  & ≈7.9%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $7.9%$ of kibbles are less than $4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fa31037-44ef-5261-8775-57dea5d4b13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079977} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2db0d3-e7ef-5209-ae1a-a5840baaf6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079978} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ddb6ca-634f-50bb-be7d-4f70f15b69c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079979} {:answer/id #uuid "011a9a4e-038d-540d-b262-c8358f98da89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079980} {:answer/id #uuid "76b5ed90-5a35-5bc0-9f5f-d7e6dcb9fc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b5ed90-5a35-5bc0-9f5f-d7e6dcb9fc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079981} :answer-field/id #uuid "9255444d-b840-571d-a6ff-ceafd8548631" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c34e831-6b73-5cf1-b4f7-78b87586c856" :question/math-academy-id "q-139110" :question/problem "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" :question/worked-solution "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070457/assets/925a72e15da6d9ace52507e88a92da4a4e82d17b073fe6d6bcb4d6c14f716da4.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c8cd0ef-9a01-5404-b0bb-8134259f8b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139241-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079983} {:answer/id #uuid "1e889f73-2643-5f46-b2e0-5efbb8514492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139241-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079984} {:answer/id #uuid "37f4bd60-f41d-5a44-b684-266c5ff3b0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139241-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079985} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2b64bc-a88b-5d4f-824e-32b151d749d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139241-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079986} {:answer/id #uuid "501af8d8-3fe4-551b-8d98-33a4585d22e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139241-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "501af8d8-3fe4-551b-8d98-33a4585d22e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139241-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079987} :answer-field/id #uuid "4efb1c77-89b0-579d-93be-6922aa6e1ddc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94b29ebf-6423-546d-bb74-f42fcde7c3c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-139241" :question/problem "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" :question/worked-solution "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070457/assets/2db3e139bdff0aabdbe352d83e1b4ffc7dbdaf3c01dd070343778910b10384ea.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distribu